---
// Sources and licences of the Gītā Reader (src/pages/sources.astro).
import { CHAPTERS, DCS_COMMIT } from "../../../scripts/gita/sources";

const dcsBase = `https://raw.githubusercontent.com/OliverHellwig/sanskrit/${DCS_COMMIT}/`;
const chapterTwo = CHAPTERS.find((source) => source.chapter === 2)!;
---
<h2>Sanskrit</h2>
<dl>
  <dt>Oliver Hellwig, <i>Digital Corpus of Sanskrit</i> (DCS)</dt>
  <dd>
    The DCS CoNLL-U release supplies the annotation for all 700 Sanskrit verses and the displayed text of chapters 3–18.
    The repository is <a href="https://github.com/OliverHellwig/sanskrit">OliverHellwig/sanskrit</a>, pinned to
    commit <code>{DCS_COMMIT}</code>. The text, annotation and dictionary are licensed
    <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/">CC BY 4.0</a>.
  </dd>
  <dt>Display and morphology</dt>
  <dd>
    Chapters 1 and 2 use Besant’s Sanskrit from the same pinned scan pages as the English, pages 11–55.
    Their Devanagari text is transliterated into IAST, retaining the printed sandhi, spacing, lines and speaker
    captions. The remaining chapters use DCS written tokens and sentence boundaries.
    The script toggle can show either IAST or Devanagari.
    <i>CONJ</i> becomes <i>CCONJ</i>. DCS-specific values such as <i>Case=Cpd</i> (compound member),
    <i>Tense=Impf</i> (imperfect), <i>Mood=Jus</i> (jussive), <i>Mood=Cond</i> (conditional) and
    <i>Formation=peri/s</i> (periphrastic or sigmatic formation) are retained and labelled.
  </dd>
  <dt>Aligning Besant’s text to DCS words</dt>
  <dd>
    Each verse is aligned globally in IAST letters, treating aspirates and vowel digraphs as single letters.
    Each DCS word belongs to the written token containing its first aligned letter, or the next aligned letter
    if its beginning falls in a gap. Words stay in source order and appear exactly once.
    A written token may therefore carry several ordered analyses.
    Validation allows two letter edits per constituent word, plus an exact allowance for homorganic nasal spellings.
    Changes away from word edges are rejected unless they are those nasal spellings or an explicit reading exception.
    Unknown differences fail the data build rather than receiving guessed analyses.
  </dd>
</dl>
<details>
  <summary>Pinned Sanskrit chapter files</summary>
  <ol>
    {CHAPTERS.map((source) => <li>
      <a href={dcsBase + source.dcsFile}><code>{source.dcsFile.split("/").at(-1)}</code></a>
    </li>)}
  </ol>
  <p>
    Chapter 2 also uses the same-commit
    <a href={dcsBase + chapterTwo.dcsFile + "_parsed"}><code>.conllu_parsed</code> companion</a>
    for the missing first half of verse 21, as described below.
  </p>
</details>

<h2>English</h2>
<dl>
  <dt>Annie Besant, <i>The Bhagavad-Gita</i>, fourth edition (Adyar: Theosophical Publishing House, 1922)</dt>
  <dd>
    Public domain, published in 1922; Besant died in 1933.
    The English comes from all eighteen discourses on
    <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Bhagavad-Gita_(Besant_4th)">English Wikisource</a>.
    Each discourse and each transcluded scan page has its own pinned revision.
    Pages are joined before numbered verses are extracted, because English sentences can cross page boundaries.
    Historical wording is retained; the chapter 8 title corrects the transcription’s “indesctructible”.
    Translator’s footnotes are omitted.
  </dd>
</dl>
<details>
  <summary>Pinned Wikisource revisions</summary>
  <ol>
    {CHAPTERS.map((source) => <li>
      <a href={`https://en.wikisource.org/w/index.php?title=${encodeURIComponent(`Bhagavad-Gita (Besant 4th)/Discourse ${source.chapter}`)}&oldid=${source.discourseOldid}`}>
        Discourse {source.chapter}
      </a>: <code>{source.discourseOldid}</code>.
      Scan pages: {source.pages.map(([page, oldid], index) => <>
        {index ? "; " : ""}
        <a href={`https://en.wikisource.org/w/index.php?title=${encodeURIComponent(`Page:Bhagavad Gita - Annie Besant 4th edition.djvu/${page}`)}&oldid=${oldid}`}>{page}</a>
        {" "}<code>{oldid}</code>
      </>)}.
    </li>)}
  </ol>
</details>

<h2>Alignment and source exceptions</h2>
<dl>
  <dt>Verse numbering</dt>
  <dd>
    Row numbers follow DCS. Besant has an additional opening question by Arjuna in chapter 13.
    Her verse 13.1 therefore has its own row, <code>0</code>, with English only.
    Every DCS verse 13.N faces Besant verse 13.N+1. The reader retains all 701 numbered English verses.
  </dd>
  <dt>Revised DCS passages</dt>
  <dd>
    Some revised syntactic sentences in chapters 1 and 2 have no verse counters.
    A checked-in table maps their exact sentence ids and text to the corresponding verses.
    DCS verse 2.21 lacks its first half in the main file: sentence <code>230721_1</code> (2.20b)
    is followed by <code>230723_1</code> (2.21b).
    Its first half is recovered from the same-commit companion sentence <code>229134_1_0</code>,
    including that witness’s seven annotated words. No replacement analyses are generated.
  </dd>
  <dt>Besant’s Sanskrit transcription</dt>
  <dd>
    The explicit <code>REPAIRS</code> table in <code>scripts/gita/lib/besant-sanskrit.ts</code> repairs:
    a duplicate double danda at 1.11 (Page 15); one shaping control at 1.22 (Page 19);
    <i>त्यक्त्वोत्तिष्ट</i> to <i>त्यक्त्वोत्तिष्ठ</i> at 2.3 (Page 29);
    the sole ASCII colon to visarga in <i>जिजीविषाम:</i> at 2.6 (Page 30);
    <i>व्ययसायात्मिका</i> to <i>व्यवसायात्मिका</i> at 2.41 (Page 42);
    and <i>रागद्वेषवियुक्तैस्तुं</i> to <i>रागद्वेषवियुक्तैस्तु</i> at 2.64 (Page 51).
    The changes at 2.3, 2.41 and 2.64 follow explicit corrections in the transcription’s SIC templates.
    Double avagrahas in 1.28 and 2.1 remain as printed.
    In 2.1, <i>कृपयाऽऽविष्टमश्रुपूर्णाकुलेक्षणम्</i> remains one written token, not two editorially separated words.
  </dd>
  <dt>Differences between Besant and DCS</dt>
  <dd>
    <code>BESANT_DCS_READINGS</code> in <code>scripts/gita/surface-alignment.ts</code> records the exact forms
    and DCS constituents. Besant’s singular forms in 1.28 face DCS plural analyses, and <i>samupasthitam</i>
    faces DCS <i>samavasthitān</i>. Other differences are <i>śyālāḥ/syālāḥ</i> (1.34),
    <i>sva/sa</i> (1.37), <i>klaivyam/klaibyam</i> (2.3), <i>bhaikṣyam/bhaikṣam</i> (2.5),
    and Besant’s nominative <i>parantapaḥ</i> against DCS’s vocative (2.9).
    Besant’s printed form is shown with the corresponding DCS analyses; the morphology is not silently rewritten.
  </dd>
  <dt>Unannotated Besant captions and a split verb</dt>
  <dd>
    <code>EXTRA_HEADS</code> lists speaker captions at 1.21, 1.24, 1.28 and 1.47 that have no DCS word rows.
    At 2.67, Besant prints <i>yanmano’nu vidhīyate</i> as two written tokens, while DCS supplies one verb word
    <i>anuvidhīyate</i>. The first-letter rule places that complete analysis on <i>yanmano’nu</i>; the DCS word is
    never counted or assigned twice.
  </dd>
  <dt>Candidate analyses</dt>
  <dd>
    Written forms with no analysis of their own (the captions above, <i>vidhīyate</i> at 2.67 and the chapter 13
    forms below) show the analyses DCS gives the same written form as a single word elsewhere in the Gītā, most
    frequent first, marked <i>candidate</i>. Captions draw first on other speaker captions, where a name is
    nominative. Only <i>tajjyotis</i> (13.17) occurs nowhere else and has no analysis.
  </dd>
  <dt>Four forms without usable annotation</dt>
  <dd>
    DCS sentence <code>231624</code> (13.11b) includes <i>proktam ajñānaṃ</i> in its text comment
    but lacks their word rows. Sentence <code>231635</code> (13.17a) similarly lacks rows for
    <i>tajjyotis tamasaḥ</i>. These four written forms remain in their text positions with candidate analyses
    where they have them. The chapter 13 companion has corrupted token-to-analysis
    assignments at these positions and is not used for recovery.
    The full English verses, Besant 13.12 and 13.18, remain alongside them.
  </dd>
  <dt>Wikisource transcription</dt>
  <dd>
    Explicit source exceptions repair malformed labels at 3.42, 6.23, 8.20, 9.14 and 18.32, and recover the
    omitted labels at 10.31 and 11.8. English 17.19 is misnumbered 20, and 18.14 is misnumbered 15;
    the adjacent Sanskrit numbers and neighbouring verses establish the intended labels.
    No English verse paragraphs are merged or omitted.
    The chapter 8 title corrects the transcription’s <i>indesctructible</i>.
    The transcription’s truncated wording in 11.25 is retained too.
    English speaker headings are not added where the transcription omits them, at 6.33 and 11.32.
  </dd>
</dl>

<h2>Word analysis and dictionary</h2>
<dl>
  <dt>Word analyses</dt>
  <dd>
    Annotated words carry the DCS lemma and morphology for that occurrence.
    A written form that joins several words lists its constituents in order, each with its own analysis.
    No grammatical analysis is inferred for the explicitly listed unannotated forms and captions.
  </dd>
  <dt>DCS dictionary</dt>
  <dd>
    The dictionary is <a href={dcsBase + "dcs/data/conllu/lookup/dictionary.csv"}><code>dictionary.csv</code></a>,
    matched through DCS lemma ids and licensed <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/">CC BY 4.0</a>.
    Despite its filename, it has tab-separated columns. Some fields contain unquoted newlines.
    Its compact meanings are split into senses at semicolons. Headwords without meanings receive no invented entry.
  </dd>
  <dt>Damaged source lemma spellings</dt>
  <dd>
    Three DCS lemmas and headwords store a vocalic letter as a halfwidth-forms code point: <code>k[U+FFB1]p</code>,
    <code>āp[U+FFDE]</code> and <code>atit[U+FFDE]</code> (at 2.15, 14.26 and 18.53; 2.70; and 13.25). The reader
    repairs them to <i>kḷp</i>, <i>āpṝ</i> and <i>atitṝ</i>; the corpus forms <i>kalpate</i>, <i>āpūryamāṇam</i> and
    <i>atitaranti</i> confirm the roots.
  </dd>
</dl>

<h2>Licence of this site’s data</h2>
<p>
  The aligned text, word analyses and lexicon are derived from CC BY 4.0 sources and are shared under the same
  licence. Citation mappings, companion-source recovery, transcription repairs and the Besant-to-DCS alignment
  are this reader’s modifications. Besant’s Sanskrit and English remain in the public domain.
</p>
